{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01V9NTZTeEGriJi6CqCRf67h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was written for **Q16 — How agents decide a task is \"done\"?**\n\n---\n\n**Key insight:** Termination can never be left to the LLM's self-report. The answer is a **4-layer orchestrator-enforced termination stack**:\n\n1. **Structured success signal** — a typed `FINAL_ANSWER` tool with a required JSON schema (not free-text \"I'm done\"). LangGraph models this as a `conditional_edge` routing to `END` when the schema is detected.\n\n2. **Independent goal verification** — for high-stakes tasks the orchestrator confirms success externally: run the test suite (code agent), execute the SQL and validate schema (data agent), check all citations are populated (research agent). The LLM's self-assessment is not trusted alone.\n\n3. **Hard budget limits in orchestrator code** — `max_iterations` (10–25 for complex tasks), `max_tokens_per_run`, and `wall_clock_timeout`. When fired, the agent emits an escalation event (not a silent abort) so work isn't lost.\n\n4. **Stuck detection** — fingerprint the last N action hashes; if the same tool call repeats 3+ times or K consecutive thought-only steps produce no action, escalate to HITL with state preserved for resumption.\n\n**Pitfalls covered:** free-text completion detection, over-generous `max_iterations=100`, and trusting structured signals without external goal verification for write-path agents."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":374,"cache_read_input_tokens":66924,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":374,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"22e4ae50-1927-431f-a3f8-db5953c15f9d","uuid":"b103ada0-c9ab-490b-be5c-da5a1157c4ca"}
